WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.830
Least privilege means providing users, devices, applications, and services only the access needed to perform authorized functions.

2
00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:15.625
It reduces the amount of unnecessary capability available if an account, device, or process is misused.

3
00:00:15.625 --> 00:00:18.998
Zero Trust applies least privilege at the resource level.

4
00:00:18.998 --> 00:00:30.741
Instead of granting broad access because a request crossed a network boundary, policy evaluates whether the requester needs this particular application, service, function, or data set.

5
00:00:30.741 --> 00:00:36.109
Permissions should reflect role and context, but roles alone can become too broad.

6
00:00:36.109 --> 00:00:45.728
Organizations may combine job responsibilities with resource attributes, device posture, risk signals, time, purpose, and approval requirements.

7
00:00:45.728 --> 00:00:52.283
Privileged access deserves stronger governance because administrative actions can affect many systems or users.

8
00:00:52.283 --> 00:01:00.575
Clear ownership, separate administrative roles, appropriate approval, limited duration, and detailed monitoring can reduce exposure.

9
00:01:00.575 --> 00:01:04.257
Service and workload accounts also require least privilege.

10
00:01:04.257 --> 00:01:16.000
Long-lived credentials and broad machine permissions can become invisible operational dependencies, so teams need inventories, ownership, review, and controlled lifecycle management.

11
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.714
Access is not a one-time design decision.

12
00:01:18.714 --> 00:01:24.791
Responsibilities change, projects end, applications retire, and temporary needs expire.

13
00:01:24.791 --> 00:01:32.853
Periodic review helps remove access that is no longer justified and confirms that important entitlements still have an accountable owner.

14
00:01:32.853 --> 00:01:38.969
Usability matters because controls that obstruct normal work can encourage unsafe workarounds.

15
00:01:38.969 --> 00:01:46.861
A well-designed program makes the appropriate path understandable and efficient while reserving additional friction for higher-risk decisions.

16
00:01:46.861 --> 00:01:50.424
Least privilege does not mean minimum access at any cost.

17
00:01:50.424 --> 00:01:58.825
It means access that is sufficient for the approved task, limited in scope and duration, and supported by policy, monitoring, and review.
